---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../../components/layout/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { COMPANY } from "../../../data/company";
import { product } from "../../../data/products";
import { mortgageLoanSchema, faqPageSchema, articleSchema } from "../../../lib/schema";

const p = product("renovation")!;

const faq = [
  { question: "What is a renovation loan?", answer: "A renovation mortgage covers both the home and the work in one loan: you borrow for the purchase (or refinance) and the renovation together, and make one monthly payment. The appraiser values the home as if the renovation were already finished, and that after-renovation value sets the ceiling - the lender uses it to confirm the total isn't too high against what the home will be worth when the work is done. Cliffco offers five: FHA 203(k) Standard and FHA 203(k) Limited (government-insured, for primary residences), Fannie Mae HomeStyle and Freddie Mac CHOICERenovation (conventional, which also cover second homes and investment properties), and VA renovation loans for eligible veterans, service members, and surviving spouses." },
  { question: "What is the difference between FHA 203(k) Standard and Limited?", answer: "Limited is for lighter, non-structural work, such as kitchens, baths, flooring, or HVAC. The cap is $75,000, and that figure includes fees and the required contingency reserve, so the budget for actual repairs is lower. No HUD consultant is required, though your lender can still ask for one depending on the scope. Standard is for bigger projects, including structural repairs, room additions, and major remodels. It requires at least $5,000 in repairs and a HUD-approved 203(k) consultant who writes up the work and inspects it as it's done." },
  { question: "What is the difference between HomeStyle and CHOICERenovation?", answer: "They're close. Both are conventional renovation loans that cover primary residences, second homes, and single-unit investment properties, and both allow upgrades FHA won't, such as a pool. On both, renovation costs are capped at 75% of the home's as-improved appraised value, and either one needs a HUD consultant once repairs pass $75,000 or involve structural work. HomeStyle is backed by Fannie Mae and CHOICERenovation by Freddie Mac. They differ on smaller points: HomeStyle gives you 15 months from closing to finish the work, CHOICERenovation 450 days, and your loan officer can price both and recommend the better fit." },
  { question: "Should I choose FHA 203(k) or a conventional renovation loan?", answer: "FHA 203(k) is often the better fit if you want a 3.5% down payment on a primary residence, or if the project needs structural work. HomeStyle and CHOICERenovation usually make more sense for a second home or a rental, for a larger renovation budget, or for upgrades FHA doesn't allow, like a pool. Neither sets a fixed credit score floor: FHA 203(k) goes by the automated underwriting decision, and HomeStyle and CHOICERenovation need a DU Approve/Eligible or an LP Accept, with manual underwriting not allowed on either. Conventional mortgage insurance can also be removed once you reach 20% equity, while FHA mortgage insurance usually can't." },
  { question: "Can I use my VA benefit for a renovation loan?", answer: "Yes. A VA renovation loan lets an eligible veteran buy or refinance a home and finance the repairs in one VA-guaranteed loan, with no monthly mortgage insurance (the VA funding fee still applies). Buying with 0% down is possible with full entitlement; refinances are capped at 90% of the home's value. The renovation budget is capped at $75,000 or 35% of the after-renovation value, whichever is less, plus fees. The loan is limited by whichever is lower: the home's appraised value once the work is finished, or what the home actually costs you (purchase price plus the renovation). The work has to be non-structural, apart from ADA and accessibility-related repairs. The home must be your primary residence." },
  { question: "How does the renovation disbursement work?", answer: "At closing, the renovation funds are held in an escrow account. As work is completed, the contractor submits draw requests and an inspector verifies progress before funds are released. This protects both the borrower and the lender." },
  { question: "Can I do the renovation work myself?", answer: "No. Self-help, or acting as your own general contractor, isn't eligible on any of the five renovation programs Cliffco offers. The work has to be done by a licensed contractor: one general contractor, with specialty trades added as the job needs them, except on 203(k) Limited, which allows up to three. The value of your own labor also can't be counted toward the home's value on a refinance. If you have a trade background and were hoping to do some of the work, talk to your loan officer early, because it changes which program fits." },
  { question: "What types of renovation work qualify?", answer: "All five programs cover kitchen and bath remodels, HVAC, plumbing, electrical, energy-efficiency upgrades, and accessibility modifications. FHA 203(k) Standard, HomeStyle, and CHOICERenovation also cover structural work. FHA 203(k) Limited is non-structural only. VA renovation is non-structural, apart from ADA and accessibility-related repairs. HomeStyle and CHOICERenovation are the two that allow a pool; FHA 203(k) and VA don't, though repairs to an existing pool are fine." },
  { question: "How long does a renovation loan take to close?", answer: "Longer than a standard mortgage, because there is more to put together first: contractor bids, a work plan, and an appraisal of the value after the work is done. Your Cliffco loan officer coordinates with the appraiser and, where one is needed, the HUD consultant. Ask your loan officer for a timeline on your specific file rather than a general estimate, since the scope of the work drives it." },
];

const schema = [
  mortgageLoanSchema(p),
  faqPageSchema(faq),
  articleSchema({
    headline: p.name,
    description: p.directAnswer,
    url: `/loans/${p.slug}/`,
    datePublished: "2026-04-15",
    dateModified: "2026-10-08",
  }),
];

import TermSheetHero from "../../../components/asym/TermSheetHero.astro";
import TermSheet from "../../../components/asym/TermSheet.astro";
import Chapter from "../../../components/asym/Chapter.astro";
import FormCard from "../../../components/asym/FormCard.astro";
import FaqList from "../../../components/asym/FaqList.astro";
import CtaBand from "../../../components/asym/CtaBand.astro";

const programs = [
  {
    name: "FHA 203(k) Standard",
    backer: "Government-insured",
    bestFor: "Major remodels, structural repairs, and additions.",
    rows: [
      ["Repair costs", "$5,000 minimum"],
      ["Down payment", "3.5%"],
      ["Min credit score", "No fixed minimum; set by the automated underwriting decision"],
      ["HUD consultant", "Required"],
      ["Time to finish the work", "12 months from closing"],
      ["Property types", "Primary residence, 1–4 units"],
      ["Luxury items", "Not eligible"],
      ["Mortgage insurance", "FHA MIP (typically for life of loan)"],
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "FHA 203(k) Limited",
    backer: "Government-insured",
    bestFor: "Lighter, non-structural updates like kitchens, baths, flooring, a roof, or HVAC.",
    rows: [
      ["Repair costs", "Up to $75,000, including fees and contingency"],
      ["Down payment", "3.5%"],
      ["Min credit score", "No fixed minimum; set by the automated underwriting decision"],
      ["HUD consultant", "Not required"],
      ["Time to finish the work", "9 months from closing"],
      ["Property types", "Primary residence, 1–4 units"],
      ["Structural work", "Not eligible"],
      ["Mortgage insurance", "FHA MIP (typically for life of loan)"],
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "Fannie Mae HomeStyle",
    backer: "Conventional",
    bestFor: "Any permanent improvement, including luxury upgrades, on a primary home, second home, or rental.",
    rows: [
      ["Renovation costs", "Up to 75% of the as-improved appraised value"],
      ["Down payment", "From 3% first-time buyers; 5% primary; 10% second home; 15% investment"],
      ["Min credit score", "Per DU Approve/Eligible; manual underwriting not allowed"],
      ["Loan limit", "Conforming / high-balance"],
      ["Property types", "Primary, second home, 1-unit investment"],
      ["Luxury items", "Eligible, including pools"],
      ["HUD consultant", "Required above $75,000 of repairs, or for any structural work"],
      ["Time to finish the work", "15 months from closing"],
      ["Mortgage insurance", "PMI"],
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "Freddie Mac CHOICERenovation",
    backer: "Conventional",
    bestFor: "Structural and non-structural remodels on a primary home, second home, or rental.",
    rows: [
      ["Renovation costs", "Up to 75% of the as-improved appraised value"],
      ["Down payment", "From 3% first-time buyers; 5% primary; 10% second home; 15% investment"],
      ["Min credit score", "Per LP Accept; manual underwriting not allowed"],
      ["Loan limit", "Conforming / high-balance"],
      ["Property types", "Primary, second home, 1-unit investment"],
      ["Luxury items", "Eligible, including pools"],
      ["HUD consultant", "Required above $75,000 of repairs, or for any structural work"],
      ["Time to finish the work", "450 days from closing; 180 days on ChoiceReno eXPress"],
      ["Mortgage insurance", "PMI"],
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "VA Renovation",
    backer: "VA-guaranteed",
    bestFor: "Eligible veterans, service members, and surviving spouses buying or refinancing a home that needs work.",
    rows: [
      ["Down payment", "0% on a purchase with full entitlement"],
      ["Renovation costs", "$75,000 or 35% of the after-renovation value, whichever is less, plus fees"],
      ["Min credit score", "640 (1–2 units); 660 (3–4 units)"],
      ["Refinance LTV", "Capped at 90%"],
      ["Structural work", "Non-structural, except ADA and accessibility work"],
      ["Time to finish the work", "120 days from closing"],
      ["Property types", "Primary residence, 1–4 units"],
      ["Mortgage insurance", "None (VA funding fee applies)"],
    ],
  },
];

const fit = [
  { project: "Cosmetic updates under $75,000 on the home you'll live in", program: "FHA 203(k) Limited" },
  { project: "A fixer-upper that needs structural work or an addition", program: "FHA 203(k) Standard" },
  { project: "Upgrades FHA won't cover, like a new pool", program: "HomeStyle or CHOICERenovation" },
  { project: "A second home or a single-unit rental property", program: "HomeStyle or CHOICERenovation" },
  { project: "An eligible veteran buying a home that needs work", program: "VA Renovation" },
];

const planFor = [
  { label: "Start the work fast", body: "Renovation has to begin within 30 days of closing on FHA and conventional programs, and within 15 days on VA. If work stops for more than 30 days, the lender can treat the loan as in default." },
  { label: "Finish on schedule", body: "FHA 203(k) Limited allows 9 months, FHA 203(k) Standard and HomeStyle 12 months, CHOICERenovation 450 days, and VA 120 days from the note date." },
  { label: "Contingency reserve", body: "A reserve of 10% to 20% of the renovation cost is required and counts inside your renovation budget. It rises to at least 15% if the utilities are off or there's water, mold, or fire damage." },
  { label: "Draws, not a lump sum", body: "Contractors are paid in stages after an inspector confirms the work is done. Each release holds back 10% until the final inspection, and funds are paid by a check made out to you and the contractor together." },
  { label: "No cash back", body: "Renovation money can only be spent on the approved work. FHA and conventional programs allow no cash back at closing; VA allows a small incidental amount." },
  { label: "Your contractor is vetted too", body: "Contractors must be licensed, bonded, and insured, and supply references. VA requires a VA builder ID number before the appraisal is issued." },
];

const steps = [
  { title: "Get pre-approved", body: "Your loan officer reviews your income, credit, and savings and points you to the program that fits your project." },
  { title: "Line up bids and a work plan", body: "Your contractor prices the work. On FHA 203(k) Standard, a HUD-approved consultant writes up the plan and the budget." },
  { title: "Appraise the value after the work", body: "The appraiser values the home as if the renovation were already finished. That figure is the ceiling: it's how the lender checks that the purchase price plus the renovation budget isn't too high for what the home will be worth." },
  { title: "Close on one loan", body: "You close on the home and the renovation together. The renovation money goes into an escrow account." },
  { title: "Renovate in stages", body: "Your contractor is paid in draws as inspections confirm each stage of the work, through a final inspection." },
];
---

<Layout title="Renovation Loans: FHA 203(k), HomeStyle, CHOICERenovation & VA | Cliffco" description={p.directAnswer} schema={schema}>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={[
    { name: "Home", href: "/" },
    { name: "Loans", href: "/loans/" },
    { name: p.shortName, href: `/loans/${p.slug}/` },
  ]} />

  <TermSheetHero
    eyebrow={p.category}
    lead="Buy a home that needs work, or fix up the one you own. Your loan covers the purchase and the renovation, and the home is appraised at its value after the work is done."
    facts={[
      { label: "Loan options", value: "5 programs" },
      { label: "Down payment", value: "From 0% (VA purchase)" },
      { label: "Appraised at", value: "Value after the work" },
    ]}
  >
    {p.name}
    <Fragment slot="actions">
      <a href="#contact" class="btn btn-primary">Start the conversation</a>
      <a href={`tel:${COMPANY.tollFreeE164}`} class="btn btn-secondary">{COMPANY.tollFree}</a>
    </Fragment>
  </TermSheetHero>

  <TermSheet>
    <FormCard slot="form" title="Price out your renovation with a loan officer." sub="Tell us the property and the scope of the project and we'll show you which program fits." formSource="loan-renovation" />

    <Chapter eyebrow="The programs" title="Five ways to finance a renovation.">
        <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text)] leading-relaxed" style="font-size: 1.0625rem;">{p.directAnswer}</p>
        <div class="mt-8 grid gap-4">
          {programs.map((prog, i) => (
            <article class={`rounded-2xl p-6 ${i === 0 ? "bg-[var(--color-ink)] text-white" : "border-2 border-[var(--color-ink)]"}`}>
              <p class={`font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.12em] ${i === 0 ? "text-white/60" : "text-[var(--color-text-muted)]"}`}>{prog.backer}</p>
              <h3 class={`mt-2 text-lg font-semibold ${i === 0 ? "!text-white" : "text-[var(--color-ink)]"}`}>{prog.name}</h3>
              <p class={`mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed ${i === 0 ? "text-white/80" : "text-[var(--color-text)]"}`}><strong>Best for:</strong> {prog.bestFor}</p>
              <dl class={`mt-4 border-t ${i === 0 ? "border-white/25" : "border-black/15"}`}>
                {prog.rows.map(([k, v]) => (
                  <div class={`flex justify-between gap-4 py-2.5 border-b text-sm ${i === 0 ? "border-white/15" : "border-black/10"}`}>
                    <dt class={i === 0 ? "text-white/60" : "text-[var(--color-text-muted)]"}>{k}</dt>
                    <dd class="text-right">{v}</dd>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </dl>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
      </Chapter>

  <!-- WHICH ONE FITS -->
  <Chapter eyebrow="Which one fits" title="Match the loan to the project.">
    <dl class="border-t border-[var(--color-ink)]">
      {fit.map((f) => (
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-[1fr_minmax(0,14rem)] gap-x-6 gap-y-1 py-4 border-b border-black/15">
          <dt class="text-[var(--color-text)]">{f.project}</dt>
          <dd class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)] sm:text-right">{f.program}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">Not sure where your project lands? A loan officer can price it more than one way and show you the difference.</p>
  </Chapter>

  <!-- HOW IT WORKS -->
  <Chapter eyebrow="How it works" title="From bid to finished home.">
    <ol class="list-none m-0 p-0 border-t border-[var(--color-ink)]">
      {steps.map((s, i) => (
        <li class="grid grid-cols-[3rem_1fr] gap-4 py-6 border-b border-black/15">
          <span class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)] pt-1">0{i + 1}</span>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">{s.title}</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-[var(--color-text)] leading-relaxed">{s.body}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </Chapter>

  <!-- WHAT TO PLAN FOR -->
  <Chapter eyebrow="What to plan for" title="The rules that shape your project.">
    <p class="text-[var(--color-text)] leading-relaxed" style="font-size: 1.0625rem;">
      Renovation loans come with deadlines and reserves that a standard mortgage doesn't have. These are
      the ones that most often catch borrowers by surprise.
    </p>
    <dl class="mt-8 border-t border-[var(--color-ink)]">
      {planFor.map((item) => (
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-[minmax(0,13rem)_1fr] gap-x-6 gap-y-1 py-4 border-b border-black/15">
          <dt class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-[var(--color-ink)] pt-1">{item.label}</dt>
          <dd class="text-[var(--color-text)] leading-relaxed">{item.body}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>
  </Chapter>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <Chapter eyebrow="FAQ" title="How renovation financing works.">
    <FaqList items={faq} />
  </Chapter>
  </TermSheet>


  <CtaBand title="Buy the house. Fund the renovation. One loan." text="A Cliffco loan officer can walk you through the right program for your project.">
    <a href="#contact" class="btn btn-primary">Start the conversation</a>
    <a href={`tel:${COMPANY.tollFreeE164}`} class="btn btn-secondary">{COMPANY.tollFree}</a>
  </CtaBand>
</Layout>
